    description:
      "Death Cafés — informal, facilitated conversations about death over tea and cake — have spread to dozens of countries; descriptive and qualitative studies find attendees value the openness and report increased death awareness and motivation to plan, while researchers note the absence of outcome trials. Death education has a longer record: meta-analyses of death-education courses found that didactic programs reduced death anxiety, whereas experiential programs sometimes increased it in the short term, and death anxiety itself is a transdiagnostic factor that tracks the severity of many mental disorders. The pairing — a low-stakes café to open the subject, then a structured course — matches what the evidence supports.",
    action: "Attend one Death Café in the next two months and, if it helps, enrol in a structured death-education or end-of-life planning course (community college, hospice or faith community). Use the sessions to complete the advance plans on this shelf.",
