    evidenceTag: "Emerging",
    impact: { magnitude: 1, latency: "months", durability: "transient", effort: "moderate" },
    callout: "Floor-rated. The only sizeable study of bucket lists is descriptive — it catalogues what people put on them — and there is no outcome evidence that writing or completing one improves well-being, meaning or end-of-life preparedness. What the adjacent research supports is narrower: experiences beat possessions, anticipation is part of the value, and enduring regrets are about the self one failed to become rather than the trips not taken. Spend the energy on those.",
    description:
      "A survey of more than three thousand adults found bucket lists were common and clustered into travel, achievements, relationships, financial goals and time with family, and proposed the list as a way for clinicians to learn what matters to a patient. That is the extent of the evidence. The experiential-consumption literature shows experiences bring more lasting satisfaction than possessions and that anticipation of experiences is itself enjoyable; regret research finds people's most enduring regrets concern inaction and unmet ideals of who they wanted to be. A list of experiences is not harmful, but as a legacy tool it is unproven, and it can crowd out the 'ought' and 'ideal' self-work that the regret evidence points to.",
